## Proof

A permutation is, by the standard exponential formula (Flajolet–Sedgewick), a **set of cycles**. The exponential generating function (EGF) for the class of all permutations is

$$P(z) = \exp\!\Bigl(\sum_{k\ge 1} \frac{z^{k}}{k}\Bigr) = \exp\!\bigl(-\log(1-z)\bigr) = \frac{1}{1-z}.$$

We now introduce the bivariate marker $u$ that marks **r-cycles** (cycles of length exactly $r$). Under the labelled construction, each cycle of length $k$ contributes the atom $z^{k}/k$ to the EGF. To mark r-cycles, we weight every r-cycle by $u$ and leave all other cycles with weight $1$:

$$\sum_{k\ge 1}\frac{z^{k}}{k} \;\longrightarrow\; \sum_{k\ge 1}\frac{z^{k}}{k} + (u-1)\frac{z^{r}}{r}.$$

Since a permutation is a SET of cycles, the BGF is the exponential of this cycle-index series:

$$F(z,u) \;=\; \exp\!\Bigl(\sum_{k\ge 1}\frac{z^{k}}{k} + (u-1)\frac{z^{r}}{r}\Bigr).$$

Using $\sum_{k\ge 1} z^{k}/k = \log\frac{1}{1-z}$, this simplifies to

$$\boxed{\,F(z,u) \;=\; \frac{1}{1-z}\,\exp\!\Bigl(\frac{(u-1)\,z^{r}}{r}\Bigr)\,}.$$

### Verification

Expand the exponential:

$$F(z,u) = \frac{1}{1-z}\sum_{k\ge 0}\frac{(u-1)^{k}\,z^{rk}}{k!\,r^{k}}.$$

Since $[z^{n}]\frac{z^{rk}}{1-z} = \mathbf{1}[n\ge rk]$, we get

$$[z^{n}]\,F(z,u) = \sum_{k=0}^{\lfloor n/r\rfloor}\frac{(u-1)^{k}}{k!\,r^{k}}.$$

Extracting $[u^{j}]$ via $[u^{j}](u-1)^{k} = \binom{k}{j}(-1)^{k-j}$:

$$[z^{n}u^{j}]\,F(z,u) = \sum_{k\ge j}\binom{k}{j}\frac{(-1)^{k-j}}{k!\,r^{k}}\mathbf{1}[rk\le n] = \frac{1}{j!\,r^{j}}\sum_{m\ge 0}\frac{(-1)^{m}}{m!\,r^{m}}\mathbf{1}[r(j+m)\le n].$$

Multiplying by $n!$ gives the number of permutations of size $n$ with exactly $j$ r-cycles:

$$n!\,[z^{n}u^{j}]\,F = \frac{n!}{r^{j}\,j!}\sum_{m=0}^{\lfloor n/r\rfloor-j}\frac{(-1)^{m}}{r^{m}\,m!},$$

which is the classical inclusion–exclusion formula for the count of permutations of $[n]$ having exactly $j$ cycles of length $r$. This confirms the BGF.

### Mean check

Setting $u=1$ recovers $F(z,1)=\frac{1}{1-z}=P(z)$, the EGF of all permutations. The expected number of r-cycles is

$$\mathbb{E}[\text{# r-cycles}] = \frac{[z^{n}]\,\partial_{u}F(z,u)\big|_{u=1}}{[z^{n}]\,F(z,1)} = \frac{[z^{n}]\,\frac{1}{1-z}\cdot\frac{z^{r}}{r}}{1} = \frac{1}{r}\quad(n\ge r),$$

matching the well-known fact that the expected number of r-cycles in a random permutation of size $n\ge r$ is $1/r$.

